(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-antibiotics-resistance-quiz"]={"title":"Antibiotics and Resistance — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does the lecture describe the structural difference of carbapenems and monobactams?","choices":["Carbapenems have a single ring; monobactams substitute a carbon for a sulphur and add a double bond","Both add a beta-lactamase inhibitor to the basic penicillin structure","Carbapenems substitute a carbon for a sulphur and add a double bond; monobactams have a single ring","Carbapenems are naturally produced; monobactams are entirely synthetic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both sit within the beta-lactam family alongside penicillins and penicillinase-resistant penicillins. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which four problems with antimicrobial therapy does the lecture group together?","choices":["Cost, availability, storage and administration route","Superinfection, direct toxicity, hypersensitive reactions and resistance","Bactericidal failure, bacteriostatic failure, poor absorption and rapid clearance","Misuse, overuse, incomplete regimens and leftover prescriptions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those four organise the whole final section of the lecture. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How does the lecture define selective toxicity, and how is it normally achieved?","choices":["Killing every microbe present in the sample; normally achieved by raising the dose above the upper limit of the therapeutic window until no organism survives","Working against the target pathogen without harming the host; normally achieved by interfering with processes or structures found in the pathogen's cells and not the host's","Selecting the narrowest possible spectrum for a given infection","Choosing a drug the organism has never been exposed to"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is why drugs against eukaryotic pathogens are harder to find. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How does the lecture define resistance?","choices":["The ability of a microorganism to avoid the harmful effects of an antibiotic by destroying it, transporting it out of the cell, or undergoing changes that block its effects","The failure of a drug to reach the target site at an effective concentration, whether through poor absorption, rapid clearance or barriers to distribution","The inhibition of growth without killing, as opposed to bactericidal action","The emergence of a secondary infection during therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three routes appear again later as the cellular and biochemical mechanisms. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Vancomycin is produced by which organism, and how does it act?","choices":["Streptomyces venezuelae; it binds the 50S subunit","Amycolatopsis orientalis; a glycopeptide that binds NAG and NAM subunits and prevents their incorporation into the wall","Paenibacillus polymyxa; a polypeptide that disrupts the outer membrane by attaching directly to its lipid components","Saccharopolyspora erythraea; it prevents amino acid translocation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls it an important “last line” against antibiotic-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which pairing of antihelminthic drug to mechanism is correct?","choices":["Praziquantel prevents ATP generation (tapeworms); niclosamide alters membrane permeability (flatworms)","Mebendazole paralyzes the worm; ivermectin inhibits nutrient absorption","Niclosamide prevents ATP generation (tapeworms); praziquantel alters membrane permeability (flatworms)","All four act by inhibiting microtubule formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mebendazole inhibits nutrient absorption and ivermectin paralyzes the worm, both for intestinal roundworms. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What did Paul Ehrlich postulate in 1909, and what drug came from that work?","choices":["That moulds inhibit bacterial growth; penicillin","That Streptomyces species produce antibiotics, work which yielded streptomycin and over twenty further agents isolated from common soil organisms","That sulfa dyes could be repurposed as drugs; sulfonamides","That drugs could be developed as “magic bullets” targeting infectious organisms without harming the host; arsphenamine (salvarsan), against Treponema pallidum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That magic-bullet idea is the same principle later formalised as selective toxicity. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How do biofilms enhance resistance?","choices":["By raising the metabolic rate of the organisms inside them so they divide faster than the drug can act, by physically excluding immune cells from the surface, and by preventing any transfer of resistance genes between the organisms held within","By preventing any gene transfer between organisms","By making the organisms bactericidal to each other","They form a physical and chemical barrier that limits dissemination and sequesters antibiotics; organisms within are metabolically less active and less susceptible; and close spatial relationship enhances resistance gene transfer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three routes are listed together. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What does alpha interferon do in viral hepatitis, and what does adding polyethylene glycol change?","choices":["It blocks viral attachment; polyethylene glycol broadens the spectrum","It induces a transition from the replicating to the nonreplicating form of the virus; polyethylene glycol gives a finite treatment duration but greater adverse effects","It promotes interferon production topically; polyethylene glycol makes it oral","It inhibits neuraminidase and so stops viral escape from the host cell; adding polyethylene glycol prolongs the half-life with no accompanying disadvantage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture presents that as an explicit trade-off. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which toxicity does the lecture attribute to chloroquine?","choices":["Seizures","Oesophageal ulcerations following oral dosing without water","Cardiovascular effects and electrolyte derangements with dysrhythmias","Aplastic anaemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It sits in the same list as the antibacterial toxicities despite being an antiprotozoal. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Quinolones and fluoroquinolones act by which mechanism, and what are they used for?","choices":["Inhibiting DNA gyrase, preventing DNA from unwinding; used for urinary tract infections and hospital acquired infections","Inhibiting RNA polymerase; used against tuberculosis","Inhibiting folic acid synthesis; used broadly","Binding the penicillin-binding proteins on the cell membrane; used mainly against Gram-positive organisms and some anaerobes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nalidixic acid is first generation; ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin and moxifloxacin are later generations. They are artificially produced. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a genetic mechanism of developing antibiotic resistance?","choices":["Random genetic mutation","Plasmid swapping during conjugation","Spontaneous reversion of the bacterial chromosome to a wild type state","Spontaneous loss of the resistance plasmid during binary fission"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The listed mechanisms are random mutation, plasmid swapping during conjugation, movement of transposons, transduction by bacteriophages, transformation, and binary fission sharing any of the above. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Two drugs are equally effective, but drug A has a higher therapeutic index than drug B. What does that imply?","choices":["Drug A has a wider margin between the dose that works and the dose that harms, so it is the safer choice","Drug A reaches the target site faster","Drug A has a broader spectrum of activity","Drug A is less likely to select for resistant organisms over the course of a full therapeutic regimen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The index is the ratio of toxic to effective dose, so a higher value means a larger gap between the two. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Broad and narrow spectrum refer to what?","choices":["The number of body sites a drug can reach","The range of doses between efficacy and toxicity","The number of distinct resistance mechanisms a drug is able to overcome in practice","The range of organisms the drug will affect — broad meaning a large range, narrow a small one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spectrum of activity is one of the listed selection criteria. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Fleming's 1945 warning concerned which specific practice?","choices":["Using antibiotics in animal feed","Combining two antibiotics that act antagonistically, so that the effect of the pair is less than either agent alone would have achieved against the organism","Self-medication with too small doses, so that instead of clearing infection the microbes are “educated” to resist penicillin and penicillin-fast organisms are bred out and passed on","Prescribing penicillin for viral illness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He describes the chain ending in someone who develops a septicaemia or pneumonia that penicillin cannot save. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Griseofulvin and tolnaftate act by which mechanisms respectively?","choices":["Griseofulvin inhibits microtubule formation during cell division, interfering with mitosis; tolnaftate inhibits squalene epoxidase and therefore ergosterol synthesis","Griseofulvin binds ergosterol within the fungal membrane and forms pores; tolnaftate blocks the synthesis of beta-glucan in the fungal cell wall","Both interfere with nucleic acid synthesis","Both act only systemically, given intravenously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Griseofulvin is used for superficial dermatophytes; tolnaftate distorts fungal hyphae and stunts mycelial growth. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Remdesivir is described as which kind of agent, acting how?","choices":["A protease inhibitor blocking cleavage of viral polypeptide precursors","An integrase inhibitor preventing viral genes from entering the host chromosome after reverse transcription","A neuraminidase inhibitor preventing virus escape from the host cell","A nucleotide analog that binds viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase and terminates RNA transcription prematurely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It has broad-spectrum activity against Ebola, respiratory syncytial virus and coronaviruses. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How does the lecture define drug clearance?","choices":["The time taken for plasma concentration to fall by half","Renal clearance plus hepatic clearance plus clearance from all other tissues","The proportion of an administered dose that reaches the systemic circulation intact","The concentration at which 99.9% of organisms are killed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That total is what determines how quickly concentration falls, and therefore the dosage schedule. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"In the disk-diffusion (Kirby Bauer) method, what does the interpretive chart correct for?","choices":["Concentration, molecular weight, and other factors affecting diffusion","Renal clearance, hepatic clearance, and clearance from all other tissues","The therapeutic window of the drug","The Gram reaction of the organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Without those corrections a raw zone diameter would not be comparable between drugs. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How does transformation contribute to antibiotic resistance?","choices":["A bacteriophage carries resistance genes between bacteria","A plasmid carrying the resistance gene is passed directly between two cells during conjugation","A transposon moves between a plasmid and the chromosome","Resistance genes are acquired from a recently killed cell and incorporated into a chromosome or plasmid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which means killing a resistant organism can still leave its resistance genes available to survivors. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Chloramphenicol and erythromycin both bind the 50S ribosomal subunit. What distinguishes their actions?","choices":["Chloramphenicol changes the shape of the 30S ribosomal subunit; erythromycin blocks the attachment of transfer RNA to the ribosome entirely","Chloramphenicol inhibits peptide bond formation; erythromycin prevents amino acid translocation from the transfer RNA to the ribosomal binding site","Chloramphenicol works against Gram-positives only; erythromycin is broad spectrum","Chloramphenicol is synthetic; erythromycin is produced by Streptomyces venezuelae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chloramphenicol is broad spectrum, erythromycin works against Gram-positives. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which environmental contributors to resistance does the lecture raise?","choices":["Environmental warming, with potential correlations to pesticide use in food production and to microplastic contamination","Ultraviolet radiation and ozone depletion","Soil acidification, heavy metal contamination of groundwater, and progressive loss of environmental microbial diversity","Deforestation and loss of microbial biodiversity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The latter two are explicitly framed as potential correlations rather than established causes. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What did Alexander Fleming observe in 1928, and who turned it into a usable therapy?","choices":["Inhibition of Staphylococcus on plates contaminated with Penicillium mould; Howard Florey and Ernst Chain produced a stable clinical form in 1939","Inhibition of anthrax bacilli by mould growth; Louis Pasteur developed the therapy","That mould cured guinea pigs infected with typhoid; Ernest Duchesne produced the drug","That Streptomyces produced over twenty antibiotics; Selman Waksman commercialised them, coined the term antibiotic, and won the Nobel prize in 1952"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three shared the Nobel prize in medicine in 1945. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Bactericidal and bacteriostatic drugs are distinguished by what, and measured by which value respectively?","choices":["Bactericidal inhibits growth, measured by the minimal bactericidal concentration (the level inhibiting 99.9% of test organisms); bacteriostatic kills organisms directly, measured by the minimal inhibitory concentration","Both kill organisms; the difference is only in the speed of killing","Bactericidal works on Gram-positives, bacteriostatic on Gram-negatives","Bactericidal kills organisms directly, measured by minimal bactericidal concentration (the level killing 99.9% of test organisms); bacteriostatic inhibits growth, measured by minimal inhibitory concentration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note the precise definition of minimal bactericidal concentration as the level killing 99.9%. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed among the desirable characteristics of an antimicrobial agent?","choices":["Favourable pharmacokinetics","Good therapeutic index","Rapid development of resistance","Low potential for hypersensitive reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists LITTLE resistance development as desirable; rapid development is the problem, not the goal. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Penicillins interfere with the bacterial cell wall in three ways. Which set is correct?","choices":["Binding penicillin-binding proteins, inhibiting transpeptidase, and producing autolysins","Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking translocation, and inhibiting peptide bond formation","Binding ergosterol, forming pores, and inducing reactive oxygen species","Inhibiting DNA gyrase, preventing unwinding, and blocking RNA polymerase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Autolysins are natural remodelling enzymes, and the damage comes from breakdown without accompanying synthesis. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Beta-lactamases and aminoglycoside modifying enzymes are examples of which resistance mechanism?","choices":["Bypass of target","Drug inactivation by enzymes","Increased efflux","Modification of the target site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is one of the routes named in the definition — destroying the antibiotic. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Echinocandins differ from azoles in which way?","choices":["Echinocandins inhibit ergosterol synthesis; azoles block beta-glucan synthesis","Both act on the fungal membrane, differing only in potency","Echinocandins are reserved for azole-resistant infections; azoles are the agents typically given by the intravenous route in serious systemic disease","Echinocandins block synthesis of beta-glucan, an essential component of the fungal CELL WALL; azoles inhibit ergosterol synthesis, acting on the MEMBRANE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echinocandins are lipopeptides naturally produced by some fungal species, given intravenously, and used against Candida and Pneumocystis. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is a superinfection, and what commonly triggers one?","choices":["An infection with two organisms simultaneously, triggered by mixed exposure during a single episode of care within a healthcare facility","A relapse of the original infection; triggered by stopping treatment early","A secondary infection acquired often following the use of broad spectrum antibiotics, which reduce normal flora and allow opportunistic infections to take hold","An infection acquired from a medical device; triggered by biofilm formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Predisposing conditions include corticosteroid therapy, leukaemia, human immunodeficiency virus, systemic lupus and diabetes. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Hypersensitivity reactions are most often seen with which drugs, and what range do they cover?","choices":["Aminoglycosides; ranging from tinnitus to deafness","Sulfonamides only; limited to rash","Tetracyclines and aminoglycosides; reactions may be immediate or delayed, ranging from photosensitivity through to permanent tooth discoloration","Beta lactam antibiotics such as penicillin and cephalosporins, and also quinolones; reactions may be immediate or delayed, ranging from hives to anaphylactic shock"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both the drug families and the immediate-versus-delayed distinction are given. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 61"}]};
